      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 54-year-old woman had a secondary occlusion of the subclavian artery proximal to the internal mammary artery, which had been used for an anterior interventricular artery bypass, and was the source of recurrent angina. A left carotid-to-subclavian bypass was performed with success. This rare complication underscores the need for careful selection and surveillance of candidates for myocardial revascularization using the internal mammary artery."},
